[38;5;252m[1m## The bug[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust exported [38;5;10mhalf2float[0m with a [38;5;10mc_ushort[0m parameter. On the C ABI a [38;5;10muint16_t[0m argument occupies a full register and the [3mcaller[23m is nominally responsible for zero-extending it, so LLVM was entitled to assume bits 16..31 were already zero. When a caller didn't zero-extend, those bits flowed into [38;5;10mh >> 10[0m, producing [38;5;10mn > 63[0m and a [38;5;10mm__mantissa[0m index far past the end — [38;5;10mindex out of bounds: the len is 2048 but the index is 29299[0m — which, with [38;5;10mpanic = "abort"[0m in the release profile, [1mkilled the process with SIGABRT[22m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C never does this. GCC truncates to 16 bits at every optimization level, so [38;5;10mhalf2float(x)[0m is [38;5;10mhalf2float(x & 0xFFFF)[0m for any register contents:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mtext
[0m[38;5;10m-O0:          mov %edi,%eax ; mov %ax,-0x14(%rbp) ; movzwl -0x14(%rbp),%eax
-O1:          mov %edi,%eax ; shr $0xa,%ax ; and $0x3ff,%edi ; and $0x3f,%eax
-O2/-O3/-Os:  mov %edi,%eax ; and $0x3ff,%edi ; shr $0xa,%ax
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fix: take the wide value and truncate explicitly, which is ABI-compatible for well-behaved callers and bit-identical to the C for ill-behaved ones.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mpub extern "C" fn half2float(h: c_uint) -> c_float {
    let h = h as u16;
    ...
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I confirmed the test is not vacuous by reverting the fix: the un-patched [38;5;10m.so[0m aborts, the patched one passes 37/37.[0m[0m
